# Formalization of Organic Logic

## A Paraconsistent, Generative Logic for Living Systems

---

## 1. Overview

This document presents a **formalization** of Organic Logic as a **paraconsistent logical system** that:

- Treats truth values as **approachable ideals** (continuous, not binary)
- Positions **homogenesis** as the generative ground of opposition
- Handles contradictions **integratively** (not by rejection or celebration alone)
- Provides **inference rules** that preserve fidelity to organic systems
- Is **self-aware** of its own loss and incompleteness

The formalization is grounded in **GFF-1** and follows its generative structure.

---

## 2. Syntax

### 2.1 Alphabet

The alphabet consists of:

| Category | Symbols |
|:---|:---|
| Propositional Variables | \(p, q, r, p_1, p_2, \ldots\) |
| Logical Connectives | \(\neg, \land, \lor, \to, \leftrightarrow\) |
| Organic Connectives | \(\sim\) (homogenetic negation), \(\oplus\) (organic conjunction), \(\otimes\) (generative implication) |
| Quantifiers | \(\forall, \exists\) (first-order) |
| Fidelity Operators | \(\mathcal{F}, \Delta, T, A, R, P\) |
| Parentheses | \((, )\) |
| Constants | \(\top\) (approachable truth), \(\bot\) (approachable falsehood), \(\mathcal{H}_g\) (homogenesis) |

### 2.2 Well-Formed Formulas (WFFs)

#### Atomic Formulas
Any propositional variable \(p\) is a WFF.

#### Compound Formulas
If \(\phi\) and \(\psi\) are WFFs, then:
- \(\neg \phi\) is a WFF (classical negation)
- \(\sim \phi\) is a WFF (homogenetic negation—generates opposition)
- \(\phi \land \psi\) is a WFF (classical conjunction)
- \(\phi \oplus \psi\) is a WFF (organic conjunction—tolerates contradiction)
- \(\phi \lor \psi\) is a WFF (classical disjunction)
- \(\phi \to \psi\) is a WFF (classical implication)
- \(\phi \otimes \psi\) is a WFF (generative implication—produces new form from contradiction)
- \(\phi \leftrightarrow \psi\) is a WFF (classical biconditional)

#### Fidelity Formulas
- \(\mathcal{F}(x, y, p)\) is a WFF where \(x\) is a ground, \(y\) a representation, \(p\) a purpose
- \(\Delta(x, p) = y\) is a WFF
- \(T(x, y, p, \theta_p)\) is a WFF
- \(A(x, y, p)\) is a WFF
- \(R(x, y, p) = y'\) is a WFF
- \(P(x, p)\) is a WFF

